blocking: test_is_internal_request looks like a mock helper but it's a line-for-line copy of the production is_internal_request defined above. Every #[test] in this module asserts against the copy, not the real function. If someone changes the real function (flips a condition, adds case-insensitive matching, changes trim semantics), the tests here stay green while production behavior silently diverges.
The fix is to let the tests call the real function. Extract the pure logic into an inner function that takes its inputs directly, make the public function a thin wrapper, and delete test_is_internal_request:
pub fn is_internal_request(context: &RequestContext) -> bool {
    is_internal_request_inner(
        context.state.config.internal_request_token.as_deref(),
        &context.headers,
    )
}

fn is_internal_request_inner(internal_token: Option<&str>, headers: &HeaderMap) -> bool {
    let Some(internal_token) = internal_token else { return false; };
    if internal_token.trim().is_empty() { return false; }
    match crate::api::auth::extract_bearer_token(headers) {
        Some(t) => !t.trim().is_empty() && t == internal_token,
        None => false,
    }
}
Then the #[test] bodies call is_internal_request_inner(Some("secret"), &headers) directly, and a real refactor of the production function gets caught.

