suggestion: Commit 3328794 trimmed the configured internal token, which closes half of the prior whitespace asymmetry. But the comparison still uses the un-trimmed wire token: the empty-check on this line trims auth_token, but ct_eq two lines down doesn't.
If the Python sender's INTERNAL_REQUEST_TOKEN env var has trailing whitespace (very common for K8s secret mounts and CI-generated env files), f"Bearer {token}" ships e.g. "Bearer secret\n". extract_bearer_token returns secret\n (10 bytes). The configured internal_token was already trimmed to secret (6 bytes). ct_eq fails on length mismatch, request silently treated as non-internal, overrides dropped, response is 200 with empty conditions and a useless warning. Same failure mode the prior fix tried to close.
Trim symmetrically (and bind once for clarity):
Some(auth_token) => {
    let auth_token = auth_token.trim();
    !auth_token.is_empty()
        && auth_token.as_bytes().ct_eq(internal_token.as_bytes()).into()
}
(And/or strip Python-side at the source: (os.getenv("INTERNAL_REQUEST_TOKEN") or "").strip(). Doing both is fine; trimming Rust-side is the safer of the two since it's defensive against any caller.)

